== 3. Secret Prayer Distinguishes You from Hypocrites

Thirdly, consider that regularly exercising yourselves#super[+] in secret prayer is what will distinguish you#super[+] from hypocrites, who do all they do to be seen by men. “Be careful not to perform your#super[+] righteous acts before men to be seen by them. If you#super[+] do, you#super[+] will have no reward from your#super[+] Father in heaven. So when you give to the needy, do not sound a trumpet before you, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and on the streets, to be honored by men. Truly I tell you#super[+], they already have their full reward” (Matt 6:1–2). Self is the only oil that makes the chariot-wheels of the hypocrite move in all religious matters. “And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites. For they love to pray standing in the synagogues and on the street corners to be seen by men. Truly I tell you#super[+], they already have their full reward.… When you#super[+] fast, do not be somber like the hypocrites, for they disfigure their faces to show men they are fasting. Truly I tell you#super[+], they already have their full reward” (Matt 6:5, 16). Thus you#super[+] see that these hypocrites look more at men than at God in all their duties. When they give alms, the trumpet must sound; when they pray, it must be in the synagogues and on the street corners; and when they fasted, they disfigured their faces to show men they were fasting. Hypocrites live on the praises and applause of men. Naturalists report of the Chelydonian stone that it will keep its power only as long as it is enclosed in gold. So hypocrites will keep up their duties only as long as they are fed, and encouraged, and enclosed with the golden praises and applause of men. Hypocrites are like blazing stars, which, as long as they are fed with vapors, shine as if they were fixed stars; but let the vapors dry up, and at once they vanish and disappear.

Closet duty shows the most sincerity. He prays in earnest who prays without a witness. The more sincere the soul is, the more it will give itself to closet duty (Job 31:33). Where in all the Scripture do you#super[+] read that Pharaoh, or Saul, or Judas, or Demas, or Simon Magus, or the scribes and Pharisees made it their practice to pour out their souls before the Lord in secret? Secret prayer is not the hypocrite’s ordinary walk, his ordinary work or trade. There is great cause to fear that the heart of a man whose whole devotion is spent among men, or among many, was never right with God; otherwise our Savior, in drawing the hypocrite’s picture, would never have made this the very look of his face, as He does in Matthew 6:5. It is well worth noting that Christ commands His disciples not to be like the hypocrites. It is one thing to be hypocrites, and another thing to be like the hypocrites. Christ does not want His people to look like hypocrites, or to be like hypocrites. Only sincerity will enable a man to make a trade of private prayer. In praying with many, there are many things that may bribe and provoke a carnal heart, such as pride, vainglory, love of applause, or the wish to make a name. A hypocrite in all his duties trades more for a good name than for a good life, for a good report than for a good conscience—like fiddlers who are more careful in tuning their instruments than in composing their lives. But in private prayer no such trade can be carried on.
